1
00:00:02,900 --> 00:00:04,290
Narrator: The Law Behind the Deal.

2
00:00:04,290 --> 00:00:06,652
Narrator: Episode 10. Risk on the Water

3
00:00:10,552 --> 00:00:14,407
Captain Cargo: Mayday! A storm off the coast, and three crates of parts are soaked!

4
00:00:14,557 --> 00:00:17,363
Kito: Then Hans pays. They were his parts until they reached us!

5
00:00:17,513 --> 00:00:27,872
Professor Lex: Not necessarily. The question is when risk passed. Once risk has passed to the buyer, loss or damage does not stop the buyer having to pay the price.

6
00:00:27,922 --> 00:00:32,413
Professor Lex: Unless the loss was caused by an act or omission of the seller.

7
00:00:36,113 --> 00:00:46,229
Professor Lex: If the contract involves carriage, and the seller need not hand the goods over at a particular place, risk passes when they are handed to the first carrier.

8
00:00:46,579 --> 00:00:53,951
Professor Lex: If the seller must hand them to a carrier at a particular place, such as the port of Hamburg, risk passes there.

9
00:00:54,301 --> 00:01:05,639
Professor Lex: If goods are sold while already in transit, risk generally passes when the contract is made. Unless the seller already knew they were lost or damaged, and kept quiet.

10
00:01:05,989 --> 00:01:14,560
Professor Lex: In other cases, risk passes when the buyer takes over the goods. Or, if the buyer delays, when they are placed at its disposal.

11
00:01:14,910 --> 00:01:23,091
Nia: And keeping the documents, like the bill of lading, doesn't stop risk passing. But the goods must be clearly identified to the contract.

12
00:01:26,791 --> 00:01:35,211
Professor Lex: One more rule. If the seller has committed a fundamental breach, the passing of risk does not take away the buyer's remedies.

13
00:01:35,261 --> 00:01:43,634
Professor Lex: So if the parts were also the wrong specification, SafariTech could still avoid the contract, even though risk had passed.

14
00:01:43,784 --> 00:01:49,549
Kito: So for the soaked crates: risk passed to us in Hamburg. That's a claim on our insurance, not on Hans.

15
00:01:49,599 --> 00:01:54,496
Professor Lex: Exactly. And that is why the insurance in the Incoterm matters.

16
00:01:57,796 --> 00:02:03,101
Professor Lex: When a contract is avoided, both parties are released from their obligations.

17
00:02:03,151 --> 00:02:10,565
Professor Lex: But damages for the breach survive, and so does any dispute resolution clause, like an arbitration clause.

18
00:02:10,565 --> 00:02:18,881
Professor Lex: A party that has performed can claim back what it supplied or paid. Both sides make restitution at the same time.

19
00:02:18,881 --> 00:02:27,496
Professor Lex: A seller refunding the price must pay interest on it. A buyer returning goods must account for any benefit it got from them.

20
00:02:27,496 --> 00:02:37,141
Professor Lex: And a buyer who cannot return the goods in substantially the condition received generally loses the right to avoid, or to demand substitutes.

21
00:02:37,291 --> 00:02:43,397
Nia: Unless that's not the buyer's fault, or the goods were used or sold normally before the defect was found.

22
00:02:46,897 --> 00:02:52,474
Professor Lex: Whoever holds the goods must look after them, even when the other side is at fault.

23
00:02:52,524 --> 00:03:06,518
Professor Lex: If the buyer delays taking delivery, or fails to pay when payment and delivery are due together, the seller must take reasonable steps to preserve the goods. It can keep them until its expenses are repaid.

24
00:03:06,668 --> 00:03:22,260
Professor Lex: If the buyer has received the goods and intends to reject them, the buyer must preserve them. The same applies to goods put at its disposal at the destination, if it can take them without paying and without unreasonable inconvenience.

25
00:03:22,410 --> 00:03:30,524
Professor Lex: Either party may store the goods in a third party's warehouse, at the other side's expense, if the cost is reasonable.

26
00:03:30,574 --> 00:03:45,898
Professor Lex: If the other side delays unreasonably, the goods may be sold, with reasonable notice. And perishable goods, or goods that are expensive to keep, must be sold. The seller keeps its reasonable costs, and accounts for the balance.

27
00:03:46,048 --> 00:03:48,818
Captain Cargo: So nobody gets to leave me rotting on the quay!

28
00:03:52,718 --> 00:03:57,085
The Auditor: Quick check. Goods are sold while already at sea. When does risk generally pass?

29
00:04:02,135 --> 00:04:03,959
The Auditor: When the contract is made.

30
00:04:04,109 --> 00:04:11,336
Professor Lex: That's Article 68. Sometimes the circumstances show it passed earlier, on handover to the carrier.

31
00:04:15,285 --> 00:04:21,323
The Auditor: And this one. A buyer rightly rejects fresh fish, and the seller cannot be reached. What must the buyer do?

32
00:04:25,373 --> 00:04:27,692
The Auditor: Take reasonable steps to sell them.

33
00:04:27,842 --> 00:04:33,954
Professor Lex: Article 88: perishable goods must be sold; keep costs, account for the rest

34
00:04:37,505 --> 00:04:39,675
Nia: Risk passed? Buyer still pays.

35
00:04:39,725 --> 00:04:42,339
Nia: Carriage: first carrier, or the named place.

36
00:04:42,389 --> 00:04:44,862
Nia: In transit: when the contract is made.

37
00:04:44,912 --> 00:04:47,306
Nia: Avoidance: release and restitution.

38
00:04:47,356 --> 00:04:49,724
Nia: Preserve the goods; sell perishables.

39
00:04:49,774 --> 00:04:59,946
Professor Lex: That completes the Sales Convention. Next time, the paperwork that moves goods and money around the world: bills of lading, and bills of exchange.

40
00:04:59,995 --> 00:05:02,932
Captain Cargo: My favourite piece of paper is the bill of lading!

41
00:05:03,282 --> 00:05:07,194
Narrator: Next time: Episode 11. Paper That Moves the World.

